const lesson=(id,level,topic,title,what,how,code,delivery,followups,followupAnswers,traps)=>({id,level,topic,title,what,how,code,delivery,followups,followupAnswers,traps});
export const jenkinsLessons=[
lesson('jenkins-001','L1','Foundations','What is Jenkins?','Jenkins is an automation server commonly used to orchestrate CI/CD workflows.','A controller schedules jobs and agents execute pipeline steps. Pipelines are usually defined as code in a Jenkinsfile and can integrate source control, tests, artifact repositories, Docker, Kubernetes and approval systems.','pipeline {\n  agent any\n  stages {\n    stage("Test") { steps { sh "./mvnw test" } }\n  }\n}','I describe Jenkins as an automation engine: the important part is the pipeline-as-code, execution model, credentials, agents and delivery controls rather than Jenkins itself.',["Jenkins vs GitHub Actions?", "Why use a Jenkins agent?"],["Jenkins is preferable when we need a centrally governed automation platform, custom agents, deep plugin integrations, or infrastructure control. GitHub Actions is attractive when the repository is already GitHub-centric and managed runners meet the security and operational requirements.", "Agents keep build and deployment workloads away from the controller and let us select the right CPU, OS, tools, network access, or isolation for each stage. I also prefer ephemeral agents for sensitive or inconsistent workloads."],["Do not describe Jenkins as only a build tool."]),
lesson('jenkins-002','L1','Architecture','Jenkins controller vs agent','The controller coordinates Jenkins workloads; agents execute pipeline work.','The controller handles scheduling, pipeline orchestration and metadata while agents provide the workspace and tools needed by a build. Keeping heavy builds off the controller improves isolation and scalability.','controller\n  ↓ schedules\nagent-1 → checkout/test/build','The controller should orchestrate, while agents perform resource-intensive work.',["Can one controller use many agents?", "Why avoid builds on the controller?"],["Yes. A controller can schedule work across many agents, usually selected by labels or provisioning rules. This lets independent jobs scale horizontally without putting build load on the controller.", "Builds consume CPU, memory, disk and can execute arbitrary build code. Keeping them on agents reduces controller contention and limits the blast radius if a build is compromised."],["Do not install every build tool on the controller unnecessarily."]),
lesson('jenkins-003','L1','Pipelines','What is a Jenkins Pipeline?','A Jenkins Pipeline is a code-defined workflow made of stages, steps, agents, conditions and post actions.','Declarative Pipeline provides a structured syntax for predictable CI/CD; Scripted Pipeline provides more Groovy flexibility.','pipeline {\n  agent any\n  stages {\n    stage("Build") { steps { sh "./mvnw package" } }\n  }\n}','I prefer Declarative Pipeline for most delivery workflows because stages, conditions and post behavior are easy to review.',["Declarative vs Scripted?", "Where should the Jenkinsfile live?"],["Declarative gives a structured, validated pipeline model that is easier to standardize and review. Scripted is more flexible Groovy and is useful when the workflow needs dynamic control flow that Declarative cannot express cleanly.", "Normally in the application repository at the repository root so pipeline changes are versioned with the code. For organization-wide behavior, I move reusable logic into a versioned Shared Library rather than hiding it in Jenkins UI configuration."],["Do not put an entire pipeline into opaque shell scripts."]),
lesson('jenkins-004','L1','Pipelines','Declarative vs Scripted Pipeline','Declarative Pipeline uses a constrained structured syntax; Scripted Pipeline is Groovy-based and more flexible.','Declarative is easier to validate and standardize. Scripted is useful for complex dynamic control flow, but excessive Groovy can make pipelines harder to maintain.','pipeline { agent any; stages { stage("Build") { steps { sh "make build" } } } }','For team-standard pipelines I start with Declarative and introduce scripting only where the declarative model cannot express the requirement.',["Can Declarative call Groovy?", "When is Scripted justified?"],["Yes. Declarative pipelines can use script blocks and call approved Groovy methods or Shared Library steps. I keep most control flow declarative and isolate complex Groovy so the pipeline remains reviewable.", "I use Scripted when the pipeline needs genuinely dynamic logic, complex iteration, or behavior that becomes awkward in Declarative. I would not choose it just because it offers more syntax freedom."],["Do not choose Scripted merely because it can do more."]),
lesson('jenkins-005','L1','Jenkinsfile','Why store Jenkinsfile in Git?','Keeping the Jenkinsfile with application code makes the delivery process versioned, reviewable and reproducible.','A change to build or deployment logic goes through the same review and history as application changes. Multibranch jobs can discover Jenkinsfiles across branches.','git\n└── Jenkinsfile\n└── pom.xml','Pipeline-as-code gives us auditability and lets the pipeline evolve with the application.',["How do you version pipeline changes?", "Can Jenkins load a shared library?"],["I version the Jenkinsfile in Git and review changes through the same PR process as application code. For shared behavior, I pin or otherwise control the Shared Library version so a library change cannot silently alter every production pipeline.", "Yes. Jenkins Shared Libraries provide reusable pipeline steps, functions and conventions across repositories. I version the library, review changes, and keep service-specific orchestration in each Jenkinsfile."],["Do not edit critical production pipelines only through the UI."]),
lesson('jenkins-006','L1','SCM','How does Jenkins integrate with Git?','Jenkins can trigger jobs from repository events or polling and then checkout the requested revision.','Webhook-driven builds are usually preferable to frequent polling. The checkout stage should identify the exact commit being built.','checkout scm\necho "$GIT_COMMIT"','I want every build tied to a specific Git revision for traceability.',["Webhook vs polling?", "How do you build a PR?"],["Webhooks are usually better because SCM events trigger builds immediately without repeatedly asking the repository for changes. Polling is useful when webhooks are unavailable or for scheduled reconciliation, but it adds load and latency.", "A multibranch or organization-folder job discovers the PR, checks out the PR revision, and runs the Jenkinsfile from that revision. I publish the result back to SCM and prevent deployment stages from running for untrusted PRs."],["Do not assume the branch name alone uniquely identifies the source."]),
lesson('jenkins-007','L1','Triggers','What Jenkins triggers are common?','Common triggers include webhooks, cron schedules, upstream jobs, manual execution and SCM-related triggers.','Use webhooks for fast source-change feedback, scheduled triggers for periodic jobs, and explicit promotions for production releases.','triggers {\n  cron("H 2 * * *")\n}','Trigger strategy should reflect the event that actually requires work.',["How do you avoid duplicate builds?", "What is quiet period?"],["I first identify whether multiple SCM events are triggering the same revision, then use correct webhook configuration, quiet periods, job-level concurrency controls, and where appropriate skip/abort rules for superseded builds. The goal is to avoid duplicate work without hiding legitimate builds.", "Quiet period delays a queued build for a configured interval so rapid successive triggers can be coalesced. It can reduce duplicate work during bursts, but it should not be used as a substitute for correct trigger configuration."],["Do not poll every repository aggressively when webhooks are available."]),
lesson('jenkins-008','L1','Stages','Why divide a pipeline into stages?','Stages create visible boundaries for build, test, scan, publish and deploy work.','They improve readability, reporting, restart behavior and failure diagnosis. Independent work can sometimes run in parallel.','stage("Test") { ... }\nstage("Package") { ... }','Stages turn a shell script into an observable delivery workflow.',["Can stages run in parallel?", "How do you retry one stage?"],["Yes, independent stages can run in parallel to reduce the critical path. I only parallelize work when shared files, environments, deployment targets, or external side effects cannot race.", "Wrap only the transient operation in a retry rather than retrying the entire pipeline. For example, retry a flaky network download a small number of times, but do not blindly retry a failed deployment or deterministic test."],["Do not hide all meaningful work inside one giant stage."]),
lesson('jenkins-009','L1','Agents','What is a Jenkins agent label?','A label selects agents that satisfy a pipeline requirement.','For example, a Docker-capable agent can be selected for image builds and a Linux/JDK agent for Java builds.','agent { label "docker-linux" }','I use labels to match workloads with the tools, CPU, architecture or isolation they require.',["Can multiple agents share a label?", "Why use containers as agents?"],["Yes. A label represents a capability or placement constraint and can match multiple agents. Jenkins can schedule the job on an available matching agent.", "Containerized agents give builds a repeatable toolchain and reduce long-lived agent drift. They also make cleanup and scaling easier, provided the container has only the permissions and host access the build needs."],["Do not assume every agent has the same toolchain."]),
lesson('jenkins-010','L1','Credentials','How does Jenkins Credentials Binding work?','Jenkins credentials can be stored centrally and injected into a build only for the scope where they are needed.','Use credentials() or withCredentials and avoid printing secret values. Restrict credential access by folder/job and prefer short-lived identities.','withCredentials([string(credentialsId: "registry-token", variable: "TOKEN")]) {\n  sh "./publish.sh"\n}','Credentials should be references in the pipeline, not literal secrets.',["How are credentials masked?", "What if a command leaks a secret?"],["Jenkins can mask known credential values in console output when they are bound through supported credential mechanisms, but masking is not a security boundary. I still prevent secrets from being echoed, passed into unsafe commands, or exposed through artifacts and process arguments.", "I treat it as credential exposure: stop further use, revoke or rotate the credential, inspect logs and artifacts, and determine where it was exposed. Then I fix the pipeline so the secret is injected only where required and never printed."],["Do not echo secrets or put them in Jenkinsfiles."]),
lesson('jenkins-011','L1','Environment','Environment variables in Jenkins','Environment variables expose build metadata and configuration to pipeline steps.','Use environment for non-secret values and credentials bindings for secrets. Avoid using environment variables as an excuse to store sensitive data in logs or files.','environment {\n  APP_NAME = "orders-api"\n  IMAGE_TAG = "${BUILD_NUMBER}"\n}','I separate ordinary pipeline configuration from secret material and inject each at the narrowest scope.',["How do you override variables?", "Where should production URLs live?"],["Jenkins environment values can come from pipeline-level, stage-level, parameters, credentials bindings, or agent/runtime context, with narrower scopes taking precedence where applicable. I avoid ambiguous overrides and make important configuration explicit in the stage that consumes it.", "A production URL is configuration, not source-code logic, so I keep it in controlled deployment configuration or Jenkins/Kubernetes configuration with access restrictions. If it is sensitive, it belongs in a secret store rather than plain environment configuration."],["Do not treat an environment variable as inherently secret."]),
lesson('jenkins-012','L1','Artifacts','What is artifact archiving in Jenkins?','Artifact archiving stores build outputs so they can be downloaded or consumed after the build.','For serious promotion flows, external artifact repositories are usually preferable for durability, retention and distribution.','archiveArtifacts artifacts: "target/*.jar", fingerprint: true','Jenkins can retain evidence, but production artifact storage should normally be a dedicated repository or registry.',["What is fingerprinting?", "Why not keep all artifacts forever?"],["Fingerprinting records an artifact-to-build relationship so Jenkins can show which build produced or consumed a file. It is useful for traceability, but it is not cryptographic proof of integrity.", "Unlimited retention increases storage cost and can retain vulnerable or obsolete binaries. I use retention policies and keep long-term release evidence in durable artifact storage according to audit and rollback requirements."],["Do not make Jenkins controller storage your only enterprise artifact repository."]),
lesson('jenkins-013','L1','Artifacts','What is fingerprinting?','Fingerprinting associates files with builds so Jenkins can track where an artifact was produced or used.','It is useful for traceability when the same artifact moves between jobs or environments.','archiveArtifacts artifacts: "target/app.jar", fingerprint: true','Fingerprinting helps answer which build produced a deployed artifact.',["Is fingerprinting a security signature?", "What is better for container images?"],["No. Fingerprinting is metadata for tracking artifacts across Jenkins jobs; it does not sign the artifact or prove that it was not modified. For integrity and provenance I use cryptographic digests and, where required, signing and attestation.", "For deployment identity, I use the image digest because it identifies immutable content. Tags are useful for humans, but a release record should capture the exact digest that was promoted."],["Do not confuse fingerprint metadata with cryptographic signing."]),
lesson('jenkins-014','L1','Testing','How should Maven tests run in Jenkins?','The pipeline should use the repository’s Maven wrapper or a pinned toolchain and publish test results.','Run unit tests early, collect JUnit XML, and fail the stage when required tests fail.','sh "./mvnw test"\njunit "**/target/surefire-reports/*.xml"','I prefer the Maven wrapper because the project controls the Maven version used by CI.',["Surefire vs Failsafe?", "Should test reports publish on failure?"],["Surefire is normally used for unit tests during the test phase, while Failsafe is designed for integration tests around the integration-test and verify phases. I publish both result sets so CI reports the complete test outcome.", "Yes. I publish JUnit results in a post or always block so failed tests remain visible even when the test stage fails. The report should explain the failure rather than being lost because the pipeline stopped early."],["Do not suppress test failures just to get a green build."]),
lesson('jenkins-015','L1','Testing','How should React tests run in Jenkins?','A React pipeline should perform a clean dependency install, lint/test checks and a production build.','Use npm ci with the committed lockfile and publish test/build evidence where appropriate.','sh "npm ci"\nsh "npm run build"','The CI environment should reproduce a clean frontend installation instead of relying on a developer’s node_modules.',["npm install vs npm ci?", "How do you cache npm safely?"],["For CI I prefer npm ci because it installs from the committed lockfile and starts from a clean dependency state. npm install is more appropriate when intentionally updating dependency resolution during development.", "Cache the package-manager download cache keyed by the lockfile and Node/npm environment, not the whole node_modules directory blindly. Never include credentials, generated secrets, or environment-specific outputs in a shared cache."],["Do not commit node_modules to the repository."]),
lesson('jenkins-016','L1','Quality','How do you add a quality gate?','A quality gate stops promotion when a defined engineering policy fails.','The gate can inspect tests, static analysis, vulnerabilities, coverage or deployment health. The policy should be explicit and versioned.','if (criticalFindings > 0) { error("Quality gate failed") }','A gate is useful only when it represents a risk policy rather than an arbitrary number.',["Can warnings be non-blocking?", "Where should security gates run?"],["Yes, if the policy explicitly distinguishes advisory findings from release-blocking findings. I document the threshold so a warning cannot silently become a production risk.", "Run fast checks early for developer feedback, then enforce release-blocking checks before publication or production promotion. The exact gate depends on the threat: dependency scanning, image scanning, secrets detection, IaC checks, and runtime controls can exist at different stages."],["Do not create gates nobody understands or owns."]),
lesson('jenkins-017','L2','Docker','How do you build a Docker image in Jenkins?','Build the image after application validation and tag it with an immutable identifier such as the Git commit.','Authenticate to the registry only for the publish stage, build once, scan, push and record the digest.','sh "docker build -t registry.example.com/orders:${GIT_COMMIT} ."','I build from a known commit and use immutable image identity for deployment.',["Tag vs digest?", "Should the agent have Docker socket access?"],["A tag such as 1.4.0 is a human-friendly mutable name, while a digest identifies exact image content. I deploy by digest or an immutable release reference and retain the tag only as metadata.", "Not by default. Access to the host Docker socket can effectively grant powerful control over the host, so I prefer rootless or isolated builders, dedicated agents, or a remote build service with narrowly scoped permissions."],["Do not use latest as the only deployment identity."]),
lesson('jenkins-018','L2','Docker','How do you push a Docker image securely?','The pipeline logs into the registry using Jenkins-managed credentials and pushes a known image.','Limit credential scope, avoid logging login output, and prefer short-lived registry tokens or workload identity where available.','withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId:"registry", usernameVariable:"USER", passwordVariable:"PASS")]) {\n  sh "echo $PASS | docker login --password-stdin"\n}','Registry credentials should exist only for the publication boundary.',["How do you rotate credentials?", "Can the agent pull private base images?"],["Create a replacement credential, update the Jenkins credential reference or secret backend, validate the pipeline, then revoke the old credential. I prefer short-lived credentials so rotation is routine rather than an emergency.", "Yes, but the pull identity should be scoped to the required registry/repositories and used only during the build. I avoid embedding registry credentials in the Dockerfile or image and keep the pull secret out of logs."],["Never hard-code registry passwords in a Jenkinsfile."]),
lesson('jenkins-019','L2','Docker','How do you scan container images in Jenkins?','An image scanner checks OS packages and application dependencies for known vulnerabilities before publication or promotion.','Build the candidate, scan by digest, apply severity policy and retain the scan result with the build.','docker build -t app:$GIT_COMMIT .\nscanner app:$GIT_COMMIT','I want the exact image that is deployed to have a recorded security result.',["Which severities block?", "What about false positives?"],["The blocking threshold should be based on exploitability, exposure, compensating controls and organizational policy, not a universal severity number. For an internet-facing production image, critical findings and relevant high-risk exploitable findings would normally block promotion.", "Validate the finding against the actual package and exploitability, then document a time-bound exception if it is genuinely non-actionable. I do not simply suppress scanner results because they are inconvenient."],["Do not scan one tag and deploy a different digest."]),
lesson('jenkins-020','L2','Parallelism','How do you run independent Jenkins stages in parallel?','Parallel stages execute independent work concurrently to shorten the critical path.','Use parallel for unit tests, linting and static analysis when they do not depend on each other.','parallel {\n  stage("Unit") { steps { sh "./mvnw test" } }\n  stage("Lint") { steps { sh "npm run lint" } }\n}','I parallelize independent checks while preserving deterministic inputs and required gates.',["What should not run in parallel?", "How do you limit concurrency?"],["Avoid parallel stages that mutate the same workspace, deploy to the same target, update shared state, or depend on each other. If two tasks need isolation, give them separate workspaces or agents instead.", "Use pipeline/job concurrency controls and deployment locks where only one release may mutate an environment. I choose the limit from the resource and consistency constraint rather than arbitrarily setting one value."],["Do not parallelize steps that mutate the same workspace or shared resource unsafely."]),
lesson('jenkins-021','L2','Caching','How do you cache dependencies in Jenkins?','Caching stores reusable dependency data to reduce network and build time.','Use Maven/Gradle/npm caches with controlled agent persistence or external caches, while keeping lockfiles and dependency verification authoritative.','Maven: ~/.m2/repository\nnpm: ~/.npm','Caching is an optimization; correctness must never depend on an untrusted stale cache.',["How do you invalidate a cache?", "Can caches leak secrets?"],["Key the cache with inputs that determine its validity, such as the dependency lockfile hash, toolchain version, and architecture. When those inputs change, the key changes naturally; I also provide a manual cache-busting path for exceptional corruption.", "Yes. A cache can accidentally persist credentials, tokens, private configuration, or generated files. I cache only deterministic dependency data and explicitly exclude secret-bearing paths."],["Do not let cache contents become an undeclared source of build inputs."]),
lesson('jenkins-022','L2','Workspaces','What is a Jenkins workspace?','A workspace is the filesystem area where an agent performs checkout and build operations for a job.','Clean or isolate workspaces when jobs can contaminate one another, especially on persistent agents.','deleteDir()\ncheckout scm','I want each build to start from a predictable workspace state.',["When should you clean workspaces?", "Can stages share a workspace?"],["Clean before or after builds when stale files could affect reproducibility, especially on reused agents. With ephemeral agents cleanup is mostly automatic, while persistent agents need deliberate workspace hygiene.", "Sequential stages can share a workspace when the handoff is intentional and deterministic. Parallel stages should generally use isolated workspaces if they create or modify overlapping files."],["Do not assume a persistent workspace is clean."]),
lesson('jenkins-023','L2','Concurrency','How do you prevent overlapping deployments?','Jenkins can disable concurrent executions or use locks around shared deployment resources.','Prevent two production promotions from racing and changing the same environment at once.','options { disableConcurrentBuilds() }','For production deployments I make concurrency an explicit safety decision.',["When is concurrency useful?", "What if two releases are valid?"],["Concurrency is useful for independent validation builds or non-conflicting environments because it improves throughput. I restrict it for production deployments and shared resources where overlapping changes can create race conditions.", "Both may be valid, but production promotion needs an explicit ordering policy. I usually serialize deployments and decide whether the newer release supersedes the older one or whether each release targets an isolated environment."],["Do not solve deployment races by relying on timing luck."]),
lesson('jenkins-024','L2','Parameters','How do Jenkins parameters work?','Parameters allow a job to accept controlled runtime inputs such as environment or release version.','Validate parameters and avoid using arbitrary shell input as a privileged command.','parameters {\n  choice(name:"ENV", choices:["staging","production"])\n}','Parameters are useful at controlled promotion boundaries, not as a way to bypass pipeline policy.',["How do you validate a parameter?", "Should production be selectable by anyone?"],["I validate parameters at the trust boundary before using them in privileged steps. For example, I restrict ENV to an allow-list, validate versions/identifiers against an expected format, and never interpolate an arbitrary parameter directly into a shell command.", "No. Selecting production should be restricted by job permissions and, for high-risk releases, an explicit approval policy. Even if the parameter offers a production choice, the pipeline must independently enforce authorization and release gates."],["Do not let untrusted users choose arbitrary commands."]),
lesson('jenkins-025','L2','Approvals','How do you add a manual approval before production?','The input step pauses a pipeline until an authorized decision is made.','Place approval after automated evidence and before the production-risk boundary; protect the job/environment with permissions.','input message: "Promote to production?", ok: "Deploy"','Automation should do the evidence gathering; approval should decide whether to cross the production boundary.',["What evidence should be shown?", "How do you prevent unauthorized approval?"],["The approver should see the exact commit and image digest, test and security results, staging health, change or release identifier, and the deployment plan. Approval should be an informed decision at the risk boundary, not just a button.", "Restrict the input step to an authorized group/identity and separate that permission from ordinary PR or CI execution. Audit who approved, what release was approved, and whether the approved artifact is the one actually deployed."],["Do not use manual approval to compensate for missing automated tests."]),
lesson('jenkins-026','L2','Timeouts','Why should Jenkins stages have timeouts?','Timeouts stop builds that hang indefinitely and release scarce agents.','Apply stage or pipeline timeouts according to expected behavior and investigate repeated timeout failures.','options { timeout(time: 30, unit: "MINUTES") }','A timeout is a guardrail, not a fix for a slow system.',["How do you choose a timeout?", "What if an external deploy is slow?"],["Set it from normal execution time plus a bounded margin based on historical data and the failure cost. I use shorter timeouts for fast checks and carefully bounded, observable timeouts for external deployments.", "First determine whether the operation is legitimately slow or stuck. I use a bounded timeout, poll a meaningful readiness condition when possible, and make the deployment resumable/idempotent rather than allowing Jenkins executors to hang indefinitely."],["Do not set an extremely large timeout just to hide instability."]),
lesson('jenkins-027','L2','Retries','When should Jenkins retry a step?','Retries are appropriate for transient infrastructure failures, not deterministic code or test failures.','Retry narrowly around network operations or flaky external services and record repeated failures.','retry(2) { sh "curl --fail https://registry.example.com/health" }','I retry only operations where the failure mode is plausibly transient.',["Should tests be retried?", "How do you avoid masking incidents?"],["Only when the failure mode is known to be transient, such as infrastructure instability. Repeating deterministic test failures can hide regressions, so I track retries and investigate flaky tests rather than treating retry as success.", "Keep retry counts low, record each retry, and fail after the defined budget. For production actions I prefer idempotent operations and explicit health verification so a retry cannot turn an ambiguous state into an unsafe duplicate action."],["Blind retries can hide real production problems."]),
lesson('jenkins-028','L2','Post Actions','What are Jenkins post actions?','Post blocks run actions after stages or pipelines based on outcomes such as success, failure or always.','Use them for notifications, test report publishing, cleanup and evidence collection.','post {\n  always { junit "**/test-results/*.xml" }\n  failure { echo "Build failed" }\n}','Post actions make failure handling and cleanup explicit.',["What belongs in always?", "How do you notify only production failures?"],["Cleanup, result publication, logs, and other evidence that should happen whether the build succeeds or fails belong in always. I avoid placing a production-changing action there because it would run regardless of the outcome.", "Put the notification condition after the production stage and check the build/deployment result plus the environment being deployed. This avoids paging on expected PR or staging failures while still surfacing production incidents."],["Do not put the primary deployment logic only in post blocks."]),
lesson('jenkins-029','L2','Shared Libraries','What is a Jenkins Shared Library?','A Shared Library packages reusable pipeline code so teams can standardize common delivery behavior.','Keep organization-wide steps such as security scans, Docker publishing or deployment wrappers in versioned libraries.','@Library("platform-lib@v3") _\nplatformPipeline()','Shared libraries reduce copy-paste while keeping application Jenkinsfiles focused on service-specific decisions.',["How do you version libraries?", "What should remain in the Jenkinsfile?"],["Version Shared Libraries in Git and control which version each pipeline consumes. For critical production paths I prefer explicit versioning or a controlled release branch so a library change is reviewed before affecting many services.", "Keep service-specific stages, inputs, deployment targets, and release policy visible in the Jenkinsfile. Reusable mechanics such as standard checkout, testing, scanning, publishing, and notifications belong in a Shared Library."],["Do not hide critical policy in an undocumented shared library."]),
lesson('jenkins-030','L2','Multibranch','What is a Multibranch Pipeline?','A Multibranch Pipeline discovers branches and pull requests in a repository and runs the Jenkinsfile associated with each revision.','It is useful for branch-aware CI because new branches can be discovered automatically and isolated from one another.','Repository\n├── main → Jenkinsfile\n└── feature/x → Jenkinsfile','Multibranch is a natural fit for PR validation and branch CI.',["How are PRs discovered?", "How do you protect main?"],["A multibranch or organization-folder configuration scans SCM metadata and creates branch/PR jobs based on discovery rules and webhooks. The job then evaluates the Jenkinsfile associated with the relevant revision.", "Require successful PR checks, review approvals, and protected-branch rules before merge. Production deployment should trigger from the protected release path rather than from arbitrary branch builds."],["Do not give every branch automatic production deployment rights."]),
lesson('jenkins-031','L2','PR Validation','How do you design Jenkins PR validation?','PR validation should provide fast feedback without deploying untrusted code to production infrastructure.','Checkout the proposed revision, run tests/quality checks, publish results and require successful checks before merge.','PR → checkout → test → scan → report','I separate untrusted PR execution from trusted deployment credentials.',["Why are fork PRs risky?", "How do you handle secrets?"],["Fork code is controlled by an external contributor and can modify the Jenkinsfile or build scripts. Running it with trusted repository secrets or network access can turn CI into a path to credential or infrastructure compromise.", "Do not expose production secrets to untrusted PR jobs. Use separate credentials and restricted agents for PR validation, then inject deployment credentials only into a trusted release pipeline after merge and authorization."],["Never expose production secrets to untrusted pull-request code."]),
lesson('jenkins-032','L2','Security','Why are Jenkins agents a security boundary?','Pipeline steps execute code, so a compromised build can access the agent filesystem, network and credentials available to the job.','Use ephemeral agents, least-privilege service accounts, restricted network paths and separate trusted deployment workers when necessary.','PR agent: no prod creds\nrelease agent: scoped deploy identity','The runner is part of the software supply chain and must be treated as privileged infrastructure.',["How can an agent be compromised?", "Why isolate release jobs?"],["A build executes repository-controlled code, so a malicious change can abuse the agent, credentials, network access, Docker socket, or workspace. I reduce the blast radius with ephemeral agents, least privilege, network restrictions, and no unnecessary production credentials.", "Release jobs have higher-value credentials and production network access, so isolating them reduces the blast radius of normal CI compromise. It also makes authorization and audit boundaries easier to reason about."],["Do not assume Jenkins itself prevents malicious pipeline code."]),
lesson('jenkins-033','L2','Security','How do you protect Jenkins credentials from pipeline code?','Credential access should be scoped to trusted jobs and the minimum required stages.','Separate build and deploy permissions, use folders/roles, short-lived credentials and protected environments.','build job → no prod credential\ndeploy job → scoped identity','I reduce blast radius by separating who can execute code from who can deploy.',["Can a Jenkinsfile access credentials?", "How do you audit access?"],["Yes, if the job identity is authorized to use those credentials. That is why credential permissions, folder/job boundaries, trusted libraries, and PR isolation matter; a secret binding alone does not make untrusted pipeline code safe.", "Review Jenkins RBAC and credential usage, retain build and approval audit logs, and monitor unusual access patterns. I also separate who can edit pipeline code from who can approve or deploy to production."],["Masking is not a substitute for authorization."]),
lesson('jenkins-034','L2','Kubernetes','How does Jenkins deploy to Kubernetes?','Jenkins can authenticate to a cluster and apply manifests, Helm charts or other deployment definitions.','Build and scan the image first, then update the deployment to the exact image and wait for rollout health.','kubectl set image deployment/orders orders=registry/orders:$GIT_COMMIT\nkubectl rollout status deployment/orders','The deployment stage should verify readiness rather than treating kubectl success as application success.',["Where should kube credentials live?", "How do you rollback?"],["Store the deployment identity in Jenkins credentials or an external secret/identity provider and inject it only into the trusted deployment stage. Prefer short-lived, namespace-scoped or otherwise least-privileged Kubernetes identities over static cluster-admin credentials.", "Use the Kubernetes Deployment revision or redeploy the previous immutable image digest, then wait for rollout and verify health. Keep the rollback tied to the release evidence so the previous version is unambiguous."],["Do not give every Jenkins job cluster-admin."]),
lesson('jenkins-035','L2','Kubernetes','How do you perform a Kubernetes rollback from Jenkins?','A rollback restores a previous known-good deployment revision when health gates fail.','Use rollout history/revision or GitOps-controlled version changes, then verify the recovered workload.','kubectl rollout history deployment/orders\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/orders','Rollback should be fast, traceable and based on a known-good release.',["When should rollback happen automatically?", "What if the database changed?"],["Automate rollback when a well-defined deployment health objective is breached and the rollback action is known to be safe. I avoid automatic rollback for ambiguous business failures or schema changes that are not backward compatible.", "A rollback of application code does not automatically roll back a schema. I design migrations to be backward compatible where possible, separate expand and contract changes, and treat destructive schema changes as a coordinated release concern."],["Do not assume application rollback can always undo an irreversible schema migration."]),
lesson('jenkins-036','L2','Kubernetes','How do you wait for deployment readiness?','A pipeline should wait for Kubernetes to report the expected rollout state before continuing.','Use rollout status plus application smoke checks and telemetry.','kubectl rollout status deployment/orders --timeout=180s','I make readiness a pipeline gate because accepting a manifest is not the same as serving healthy traffic.',["Readiness vs rollout status?", "What if pods are Ready but users fail?"],["Readiness says whether individual pods should receive traffic; rollout status describes whether the Deployment has reached its desired revision and available replicas. I use rollout status for deployment completion and readiness/health signals for user-facing validation.", "Readiness only proves the configured readiness condition passed. I then check ingress/load-balancer behavior, application logs, dependency health, error rates, latency, and real smoke tests from the user path."],["Do not rely only on process startup."]),
lesson('jenkins-037','L3','Delivery','How do you implement build once, deploy many?','Build once, create an immutable artifact, then promote that exact artifact through environments.','The CI job produces a JAR/image once; staging and production consume the same version or digest with environment-specific configuration.','build → image@sha256:X\n      ↘ staging\n      ↘ production','This is one of my strongest controls against environment drift.',["How do you prove the same image?", "How do you roll back?"],["Record the immutable image digest after build and carry that digest through staging and production. The deployment evidence should show that the digest in the production workload exactly matches the tested artifact.", "Keep previous immutable image digests and deployment revisions, then redeploy the last known-good digest. Verify rollout and user-facing health after the rollback instead of assuming the command succeeded."],["Do not rebuild the same commit separately for production."]),
lesson('jenkins-038','L3','Delivery','How do you implement blue-green deployment with Jenkins?','Blue-green keeps two production versions and switches traffic between them after validation.','Deploy green, run smoke/verification checks, switch the routing target, observe, then keep blue available for rapid rollback.','blue: v41\ngreen: v42\nroute → green','The traffic switch is the release boundary; rollback can switch back if the new version is unhealthy.',["How do you validate green without traffic?", "What about database compatibility?"],["Run synthetic smoke tests directly against the green service through an internal route or temporary endpoint and validate dependencies, logs, metrics, and key business flows. Only switch traffic after those checks pass.", "Blue-green requires both application versions to work with the shared schema during the transition. I use backward-compatible migrations and delay destructive cleanup until the old version is no longer serving traffic."],["Do not delete the old environment immediately after switching traffic."]),
lesson('jenkins-039','L3','Delivery','How do you implement canary delivery with Jenkins?','Canary releases expose a new version to a small traffic slice before wider promotion.','Jenkins deploys the candidate and changes traffic incrementally while monitoring error rate, latency and business signals.','5% → 25% → 50% → 100%','Canary is controlled risk reduction, not just a percentage setting.',["Which metrics stop promotion?", "How long should each stage run?"],["I use service-level error rate, latency, saturation, restart/OOM signals, and relevant business metrics, with thresholds based on the service SLO. A single noisy metric should not automatically roll back without a clear policy and sufficient observation.", "Long enough to collect meaningful traffic and telemetry for the risk profile, not a fixed universal duration. High-volume services may need minutes; low-volume services may need a defined request count or longer observation window."],["Do not promote based only on pod readiness."]),
lesson('jenkins-040','L3','Database','How should database migrations fit into CI/CD?','Database changes need backward-compatible sequencing because old and new application versions may overlap.','Prefer expand-and-contract: add compatible schema, deploy code, migrate/backfill, then remove obsolete structures later.','expand → deploy → migrate → contract','I treat schema changes as part of release architecture rather than an incidental deploy command.',["Who owns migrations?", "How do you rollback a schema change?"],["The application team should own migration correctness because it understands the domain and compatibility requirements, while platform/DB teams can provide guardrails. CI/CD should execute migrations through a controlled, auditable step rather than letting every service run arbitrary DBA commands.", "Prefer forward-fix or backward-compatible migration strategies over destructive rollback. For risky changes I use expand/contract, backups, and a tested recovery plan because reversing data transformations may be impossible without loss."],["Do not assume every DDL change is safely reversible."]),
lesson('jenkins-041','L3','Microservices','How do you design Jenkins CI/CD for microservices?','Each service should build and deploy independently while shared platform controls keep delivery consistent.','Use reusable pipeline libraries/templates, service-specific tests and images, contract checks, independent deployment and shared observability/security standards.','service-A → pipeline-A → deploy-A\nservice-B → pipeline-B → deploy-B','Independent deployability is the goal; central standards should not force unnecessary coupling.',["How do you test contracts?", "How do you coordinate shared libraries?"],["Use consumer-driven contract tests or schema compatibility checks so consumers validate the provider contract before deployment. Run them in PR/CI and retain the contract version as release evidence.", "Keep the library API stable, version releases, test the library independently, and upgrade services deliberately. A compatibility matrix or automated consumer tests helps prevent one shared-library change from breaking many pipelines."],["Do not create one pipeline that deploys every service for every change."]),
lesson('jenkins-042','L3','Monorepo','How do you handle a monorepo in Jenkins?','A monorepo can use path-aware detection to build and test affected components while retaining integration validation.','Detect changed directories, run component pipelines selectively, and run broader checks when shared code changes.','changes: services/orders/**\n→ build orders','Optimization is safe only when dependency boundaries are explicit.',["What if shared code changes?", "How do you avoid missing dependencies?"],["The pipeline should identify affected services from dependency relationships and run the relevant build/test set. Shared-code changes should trigger broader validation than a change isolated to one service.", "Make dependencies explicit in build manifests, lockfiles, module metadata, and repository tooling rather than relying on workspace state. For monorepos, use dependency graphs or change detection plus a safety net of broader CI checks."],["Do not rely on simple path matching when dependency graphs are complex."]),
lesson('jenkins-043','L3','Release','How do you make Jenkins releases traceable?','A release should connect commit, build, artifact digest, deployment revision and approver.','Record Git SHA, Jenkins build ID, artifact metadata, registry digest and Kubernetes revision in release evidence.','SHA → build 842 → image digest → deployment rev 19','Traceability lets us answer exactly what is running and how it reached production.',["What should be retained?", "How does this help incidents?"],["Retain the source commit, Jenkins build, test and scan results, artifact/image digest, SBOM/provenance, approval, deployment revision, and relevant logs according to audit and incident requirements. Retention should be long enough to reconstruct a production release.", "It lets us trace a bad production workload back to the exact source, pipeline run, artifact, security evidence, approval, and deployment. That shortens diagnosis and makes rollback to a known-good release deterministic."],["Do not rely on a mutable tag as the only audit record."]),
lesson('jenkins-044','L3','Observability','How should Jenkins react to deployment health signals?','Deployment automation should consume health evidence and pause, promote or roll back according to explicit policy.','Combine rollout status with error rate, latency, saturation and business checks where appropriate.','deploy → observe → gate → promote/rollback','The pipeline should make release decisions from production evidence rather than from command exit codes alone.',["Where do metrics come from?", "How do you avoid false positives?"],["Metrics normally come from the deployed workload and platform telemetry, such as Prometheus/OpenTelemetry-based signals, load balancers, Kubernetes, and business monitoring. Jenkins should consume a well-defined release-health signal rather than inventing application metrics itself.", "Use stable signals, sufficient observation windows, baseline comparisons, and multiple correlated indicators. I also define what should happen when telemetry is unavailable instead of treating missing data as healthy."],["Do not make an automated rollback rule more fragile than the release itself."]),
lesson('jenkins-045','L3','Reliability','How do you make Jenkins pipelines idempotent?','An idempotent pipeline can be safely retried without creating duplicate or conflicting side effects.','Use immutable artifacts, declarative deployment state, unique release identifiers and commands that converge on the desired state.','kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml\n# desired state','Retries are safe when the underlying operation converges instead of blindly repeating side effects.',["What about database migrations?", "How do you handle external APIs?"],["Make migrations idempotent where practical and structure them so rerunning a failed pipeline does not corrupt state. For non-idempotent operations, use migration tooling with recorded versions and explicit locking/transaction behavior.", "Use idempotency keys or provider-supported deduplication for commands, bounded retries for transient failures, and explicit reconciliation for ambiguous outcomes. Never assume a timeout means the remote operation did not happen."],["Do not retry non-idempotent business commands without an idempotency strategy."]),
lesson('jenkins-046','L3','Supply Chain','How do you sign artifacts or images from Jenkins?','Signing attaches verifiable provenance or integrity metadata to a release artifact.','Build and scan the artifact, sign it using protected signing infrastructure, publish provenance and verify signatures at deployment time where supported.','build → scan → sign → registry → verify','Signing strengthens trust in what was built and what is allowed to run.',["Where should signing keys live?", "What is provenance?"],["Keep signing keys in a dedicated KMS/HSM or secret-management system with strict access controls, ideally using short-lived signing identities. The Jenkins agent should receive only the capability needed for the signing operation and not a reusable private key in the workspace.", "Provenance records how an artifact was produced: source revision, builder, inputs, toolchain, and build process. It lets us verify that a production artifact came from an approved source and build path."],["Do not store long-lived signing private keys in the Jenkinsfile or workspace."]),
lesson('jenkins-047','L3','Supply Chain','What is SBOM generation in CI/CD?','An SBOM lists software components and versions contained in an artifact.','Generate it during build/release, retain it with the artifact and use it for vulnerability and license response.','build → SBOM → scan → publish','An SBOM improves visibility into what the application actually contains.',["CycloneDX vs SPDX?", "Does an SBOM prevent vulnerabilities?"],["Both are widely used SBOM formats. I choose based on the tools and ecosystem we integrate with, then standardize one format for consistent ingestion, policy checks, and retention rather than treating the format choice as the security control.", "No. An SBOM inventories components and versions; it does not remove vulnerabilities. It improves detection, impact analysis, and response by letting us map a vulnerability to affected artifacts quickly."],["An SBOM is inventory, not a security fix."]),
lesson('jenkins-048','L3','Governance','How do you separate CI from production deployment permissions?','Build validation and production promotion can use different trust boundaries and identities.','PR/build jobs should have no production credentials; a protected release job or environment receives narrowly scoped deployment rights.','untrusted PR → CI\ntrusted release → production','Separating permissions limits the blast radius of malicious or compromised build code.',["How do approvals fit?", "Can the same controller host both?"],["Approvals should sit at the production risk boundary after automated evidence is complete. They should authorize a specific release identity, not give the pipeline a general bypass around tests or security controls.", "It can, but I would isolate permissions, jobs, agents, credentials, and network paths so ordinary CI cannot reach production. For higher-risk environments, separate controllers or stronger platform isolation may be justified."],["Do not expose production credentials to every job for convenience."]),
lesson('jenkins-049','L3','Performance','How do you troubleshoot a slow Jenkins pipeline?','Start by measuring queue time, stage duration, agent provisioning, dependency downloads and external service latency.','Use stage timings and build logs to find the critical path, then parallelize, cache, right-size agents or remove unnecessary work.','queue 4m → tests 12m → image 8m\noptimize the dominant stage','I measure before optimizing so the change targets the real bottleneck.',["Queue time vs execution time?", "What if tests are inherently slow?"],["Queue time measures how long work waits for an executor or agent; execution time measures the work itself. I inspect them separately because adding agents can fix queue time while doing nothing for a slow build stage.", "Profile the test suite, parallelize safe tests, split fast PR checks from slower scheduled or release checks, and cache deterministic dependencies. I would not hide slow tests behind retries just to improve pipeline duration."],["Do not optimize a non-critical stage while ignoring the dominant bottleneck."]),
lesson('jenkins-050','L3','Troubleshooting','Jenkins build passes locally but fails on the agent. What do you check?','The first suspects are tool versions, environment variables, filesystem assumptions, network access and dependency availability.','Compare Java/Node/Maven versions, lockfiles, OS/architecture, permissions and external dependencies; reproduce using the same agent image.','java -version\nnode --version\n./mvnw -version','I compare environments before changing application logic.',["Why does a wrapper help?", "How do you make agents reproducible?"],["A Maven wrapper pins the Maven distribution expected by the project, reducing differences between developer and CI environments. It does not pin Java or the OS, so those still need controlled agent images/toolchains.", "Use versioned agent images or infrastructure definitions that pin the OS, JDK/Node/Maven and required tooling. Recreate agents rather than manually patching long-lived machines."],["Do not add retries to deterministic environment failures."]),
lesson('jenkins-051','L3','Troubleshooting','Jenkins agent keeps going offline. How do you investigate?','Check connectivity, agent process logs, resource pressure, workspace/storage failures and controller-agent compatibility.','Look for CPU/memory exhaustion, disk pressure, network interruptions and provisioning failures before changing pipeline code.','df -h\nfree -m\n# inspect agent logs','Agent instability is an infrastructure problem until evidence shows otherwise.',["What if only Docker builds fail?", "Why use ephemeral agents?"],["Compare Docker daemon/buildkit availability, registry access, credentials, filesystem permissions, architecture, disk space, and Dockerfile context. If application tests pass but image builds fail, I focus on the container build boundary rather than changing application code.", "Ephemeral agents start from a known image for each workload and disappear afterward, reducing tool drift and persistent workspace contamination. They also limit the persistence of an agent compromise."],["Do not repeatedly reconnect an unhealthy agent without finding the root cause."]),
lesson('jenkins-052','L3','Troubleshooting','A Jenkins pipeline is stuck waiting for an executor. Why?','The requested agent may be unavailable, labels may match no nodes, executors may be saturated or concurrency restrictions may block the build.','Inspect queue reasons, node labels, executor counts and running jobs.','Jenkins queue → why waiting?\nnode labels → capacity','I distinguish queue capacity from pipeline logic before changing the job.',["How do you add capacity?", "Can one label map to many agents?"],["First identify whether the bottleneck is executor count, matching labels, CPU/memory, provisioning limits, or a shared external dependency. Then add appropriately sized agents or autoscaling rather than simply increasing executors on an already saturated node.", "Yes. Multiple agents can advertise the same label, and Jenkins can schedule matching jobs on any available one. This is a common way to scale a homogeneous workload pool."],["Do not blindly increase executors on an undersized machine."]),
lesson('jenkins-053','L3','Troubleshooting','Jenkins deploy says success but the application is broken. What happened?','The deployment command can succeed while the workload is unhealthy or downstream dependencies are failing.','Check rollout status, pod events, application logs, readiness, metrics and smoke tests; stop promotion or rollback based on evidence.','kubectl rollout status deployment/api\nkubectl get pods\nkubectl logs','A successful command is only one signal; release success requires application health.',["What if Kubernetes is healthy?", "How do you separate release from dependency failure?"],["A healthy cluster and pods do not prove the user path works. Check Service endpoints, Ingress/load balancer, DNS, TLS, application dependencies, response codes, latency, and an end-to-end smoke test.", "Compare deployment health with dependency telemetry and reproduce the failing request path. If the new workload is healthy but a downstream service or external provider is failing, record that separately and avoid rolling back a good release without evidence."],["Do not rollback blindly without identifying the failing boundary."]),
lesson('jenkins-054','L3','Architecture','How would you design a production Jenkins platform?','A production platform should be highly available enough for business needs, secure, scalable and observable.','Use a managed or redundant controller strategy where appropriate, ephemeral agents, external artifact storage, backups, RBAC, secrets integration, audit logs and monitored pipelines.','SCM → Jenkins controller → ephemeral agents\n              ↓\n registry / Kubernetes / telemetry','I design Jenkins as a delivery platform with isolated execution and external durable systems.',["What needs backup?", "How do you scale agents?"],["Back up Jenkins configuration, credentials metadata/secret references, job definitions, Shared Libraries, plugin/version information, and build/release evidence according to recovery requirements. Secrets should be backed up through the secret-management system rather than copied casually into Jenkins backups.", "Provision ephemeral agents from a versioned image and scale them based on queue demand while enforcing CPU/memory and concurrency limits. Kubernetes-based Jenkins agents are a common way to make this elastic."],["Do not make one persistent controller disk the only copy of critical release evidence."]),
lesson('jenkins-055','L3','Architecture','How do you migrate freestyle jobs to Pipeline-as-Code?','Freestyle jobs hide configuration in the Jenkins UI and become difficult to review and reproduce.','Inventory jobs, extract commands and credentials, create Jenkinsfiles, validate in parallel, then retire the old jobs after equivalent evidence exists.','freestyle → Jenkinsfile → PR review → parallel validation → cutover','I migrate incrementally and compare outcomes before deleting legacy jobs.',["How do you avoid downtime?", "What belongs in shared libraries?"],["Migrate one job at a time or run old and new pipelines in parallel against the same revision, compare outputs, then cut traffic over. Keep the legacy job available until the new pipeline has produced equivalent evidence.", "Put stable reusable mechanics and organization-wide guardrails there: standard testing, scanning, image publishing, notifications, and deployment helpers. Keep service-specific release decisions and configuration in the repository Jenkinsfile."],["Do not rewrite hundreds of jobs without preserving behavior and evidence."]),
lesson('jenkins-056','L3','Architecture','Jenkins vs GitHub Actions: how do you choose?','Both automate CI/CD, but the operational model and ecosystem differ.','Consider existing infrastructure, Git hosting, plugin/integration needs, runner control, governance, maintenance and team expertise.','GitHub-native workflow ↔ Jenkins platform','I choose based on organizational constraints and required control rather than assuming one tool is universally better.',["When is Jenkins preferable?", "Can they coexist?"],["Jenkins is often preferable when an organization needs extensive custom integrations, self-hosted execution, complex agent topologies, legacy tooling, or centralized pipeline governance. The choice should be based on required control and operating cost, not familiarity alone.", "Yes. Teams can use GitHub Actions for repository-native checks and Jenkins for specialized enterprise deployment workflows, or migrate gradually. The important part is clear ownership and avoiding two systems independently deploying the same production target."],["Do not evaluate tools only by feature count."]),
lesson('jenkins-057','L3','Production','Design an end-to-end Jenkins pipeline for React + Spring Boot + Kubernetes','The system needs PR validation, secure image creation, staging deployment and controlled production promotion.','PR checks → merge → backend/frontend builds → tests/scans → immutable images → registry → staging → smoke/telemetry → approval/progressive production → rollback.','React CI → dist\nSpring CI → JAR → image\nimages → registry → Kubernetes','I separate fast PR validation from trusted release permissions and promote immutable artifacts.',["How do you handle frontend hosting?", "How do you coordinate two images?"],["Build the React application once and publish the static artifact to the chosen hosting platform or object storage/CDN. The release record should tie the frontend artifact/version to the same commit and promotion flow as the backend when they are released together.", "Build and test both images from the same release commit, record both immutable digests, deploy them as one release manifest/version, and promote or roll back the pair consistently. If they can be deployed independently, define compatibility contracts instead of forcing unnecessary synchronization."],["Do not rebuild different artifacts for staging and production."]),
];
